tion","reading_order":15,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"opposite of what is to be avoided, qua something bad and to be avoided, is","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,931,1346,968],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0012","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":16,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"good. So necessarily pleasure is something good. Speusippus’ method of","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,977,1344,1014],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0013","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":17,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"refuting this argument fails, i.e. [his observation] that the larger is opposed","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1022,1345,1059],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0014","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":18,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"to the smaller and the equal. For he [Speusippus] would not say that","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1068,774,1105],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0015","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":19,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"pleasure is essentially something bad.","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1149,1347,1190],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0016","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"start","reading_order":20,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"Recall Eudoxus’ argument, which I labelled ‘B’ above, as found in","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1198,342,1240],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[{"bbox":null,"end_char":2,"semantic_role":"citation","span_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0017-visual-span-000","start_char":0,"styles":["italic"],"target_id":null,"text":"NE"}],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0017","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":21,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"NE 10. 2:","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[214,1282,1329,1324],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0018","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"standalone","reading_order":22,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"(i) The opposite of something to be avoided is to be pursued.","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[202,1331,887,1373],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0019","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"standalone","reading_order":23,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"(ii) Pain is universally to be avoided.","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[190,1381,865,1423],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0020","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"standalone","reading_order":24,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"(iii) Pleasure is the opposite of pain.","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[193,1431,966,1473],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0021","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"standalone","reading_order":25,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"(iv) Pleasure is universally to be pursued.","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1515,1346,1557],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0022","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"start","reading_order":26,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"Let us call B(i) the ‘opposites premiss’. It remains neutral on the","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1565,1347,1607],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0023","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":27,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"precise identity of what is to be pursued and what is to be avoided,","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1615,1347,1657],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0024","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":28,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"but merely points out a relationship between whichever candidates","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1664,1347,1706],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[{"bbox":null,"end_char":51,"semantic_role":"citation","span_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0025-visual-span-000","start_char":49,"styles":["italic"],"target_id":null,"text":"NE"}],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0025","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":29,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"are offered. Speusippus’ strategy as reported in NE 7. 13 is as","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1714,1346,1756],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0026","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":30,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"follows. He accepts the opposites premiss, at least for dialectical","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1764,1348,1806],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0027","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":31,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"purposes, but proceeds to show that on its basis B(iv) does not","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1814,1346,1856],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0028","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":32,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"necessarily follow. Or, perhaps more precisely, he shows that B(iv)","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1864,1347,1906],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0029","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":33,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"no more follows than an alternative conclusion which would not","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1914,1347,1956],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0030","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":34,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"be to the liking of the hedonist Eudoxus. He points out in reaction","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,1993,1347,2026],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0031","note_id":"warren-osap36-pdf017-note-27","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":35,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"clear that Aristippus’ grandfather, also called Aristippus, was a hedonist at all, let","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,2030,1344,2064],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[{"bbox":null,"end_char":81,"semantic_role":"title","span_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0032-visual-span-000","start_char":71,"styles":["italic"],"target_id":null,"text":"Speusippus"}],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"warren-osap36-pdf018-native-0032","note_id":"warren-osap36-pdf017-note-27","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":36,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"alone an explicit advocate of hedonism. See D.L. 4. 4–5 with L. Tarán, Speusippus","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[151,2067,1346,2100],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":